  "answer": "The patient's presentation of pancytopenia, splenomegaly, and a 'dry tap' during bone marrow aspiration is highly characteristic of Hairy Cell Leukemia (HCL). In HCL, the bone marrow often undergoes significant fibrosis, which leads to the inability to aspirate adequate material (dry tap). The characteristic 'hairy' cells found in the peripheral blood and bone marrow are classically identified using the Tartrate-Resistant Acid Phosphatase (TRAP) stain.",
  "justification": "The clinical triad of pancytopenia, splenomegaly, and a dry tap on bone marrow aspiration strongly suggests Hairy Cell Leukemia. HCL is a rare B-cell lymphoproliferative disorder. The diagnosis is supported by the presence of cells with cytoplasmic projections ('hairy' cells) that stain positive with Tartrate-Resistant Acid Phosphatase (TRAP). Other options like Prussian Blue (iron), Ziehl-Neelsen (acid-fast bacilli), and PAS (glycogen) do not match this clinical profile.",
